"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, and absence of lymphovascular invasion. The pathological stage was pT2 with no extraprostatic extension. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 9.5 and the Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3) pathology. Furthermore, the absence of lymph node removal means nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty. Biopsy results showed a progression from Gleason 0+0 to 3+4 over time. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly limits the certainty of recurrence risk. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
